# Overview
#
# `once` means one time (`the job runs once`), but it also means after
# or when (`the write starts once the lock is held`). In the middle of a
# sentence, `runs once` reads as a complete count until the clause after
# it arrives, so the reader has to reread. A clause is a subject and its
# verb, such as `the tests pass`. The fix writes `after` or `when`. The
# rule sits at Recommended, because `once` before `this` or `that` can
# still be a count.
#
# Expression
#
# The rule counts `once` where it opens a clause, before a determiner, a
# pronoun, or a proper noun. A determiner is a word that introduces a
# noun, such as `the`, `a`, or `this`. A proper noun is a name that
# takes a capital letter, such as `Kubernetes`. Weir has no lookbehind,
# so the filter consumes the token before `once` and narrows the finding
# back to `once`.
#
# False Positives
#
# `a`, `an`, `every`, `each`, `per`, `more`, and `again` after `once`
# stay out, because each makes `once` a count (`once a day`, `once
# more`), and so do `or`, `and`, `in`, and `for` (`once or twice`, `once
# in a while`). A `once` that opens a sentence or follows a comma stays
# out too, because there it can only open a clause.
#
# `once` before `this` or `that` can still be a count (`the job runs
# once this week`). The rule accepts that misfire.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 83 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled
# hits meant after or when.
expr main <(!(,)( )once( )<[DET, PRON, PROPN], ![a, an, every, each, per, more, again, or, and, in, for]>), once>
let message "`once`. Write `after` or `when`."
let description "Flags `once` where it opens a clause in the middle of a sentence, where it also reads as one time."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Once the lock is held, the write starts."
allows "The job runs once a day."
allows "The hook runs once for each file."
allows "The write starts, once the lock is held."
